---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Hand-authored rather than markdown: this page reuses the six diagrams
// already embedded in the guides (site/public/diagrams/*.svg), told in
// one narrative pass instead of split across four documents. Keeping it
// as a standalone .astro page (like index.astro) rather than a content-
// collection doc means it costs nothing against the prose budget in
// scripts/check-purity.sh — see lib/sections.ts for how it reaches nav.
---
<Layout title="Architecture" description="How Kratos, Hydra, the portal, and the edge fit together in Othala, told through six diagrams: trust boundary, OIDC login, forward-auth, group binding, session revocation, and the first credential.">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="page-eyebrow">Othala</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Architecture</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Six diagrams, in the order a request actually crosses them: who is
      inside the trust boundary, how a login becomes a token, how an app
      with no OIDC gets an answer anyway, how one group becomes two
      different grants, what actually happens on sign-out, and how the
      first credential gets in the door at all. Each one carries the WHAT
      as its caption and its enforcer; what follows here is the WHY.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article>
    <h2>One trust domain</h2>
    <p>
      Everything downstream of a login trusts whatever token or session
      Othala hands it, so the question worth answering first is what can
      reach the two Ory admin APIs at all. The answer is one process: the
      app runs a public SSR listener the edge reaches and a second,
      internal-network admin listener (PORTAL_ADMIN_BIND_HOST) the edge never touches, so every
      admin-API caller stays inside one trust domain instead of becoming a
      service-to-service authorization problem in its own right.
      <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § Trust boundary has the enforcement;
      the <a href="/guides/hardening/">Hardening</a> guide's Admin-API
      isolation section walks the control end to end.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/trust-boundary.svg" alt="A browser reaches one hostname at the edge proxy, which terminates TLS. Inside one trust domain the admin APIs never leave: the app runs two listeners, a public SSR listener the edge reaches and an admin listener bound to PORTAL_ADMIN_BIND_HOST that the edge never touches; Kratos and Hydra each expose the edge a public API — self-service and sessions for Kratos, authorize, token and discovery for Hydra — while the app alone calls their admin APIs. Kratos and Hydra each keep a separate Postgres database. Admin APIs are reachable only from the app, never from the edge, enforced by scripts/render-config.sh, requireAdminSession, and pinned by d-admin-aal2-stepup.spec.ts and f-aal2-bypass.spec.ts.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">Three ports on the network; the admin APIs are not among them</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>How a login becomes a token</h2>
    <p>
      A relying party redirects the browser to Hydra, and Hydra ships no
      login UI of its own, so it opens a challenge back on the app for
      every piece the standard leaves to the integrator. The app gates the
      identity, Kratos runs the passkey ceremony, and the app gates again
      at consent before composing the claims a token actually carries. A
      remembered subject skips the passkey step but never the gate:
      Hydra's memory of who logged in before is never trusted without a
      live Kratos session behind it. The <a href="/guides/per-app-oidc/">Per-app OIDC</a> guide's The shape
      section walks one relying party through the whole exchange; <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The OIDC path has the sequencing.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/oidc-login-path.svg" alt="A sequence across four lifelines — browser, Hydra, the Othala app and Kratos — for an example relying party, Grafana. The browser is redirected to Hydra's authorize endpoint, which opens a login challenge on the app; with no session yet the app bounces the browser to Kratos, which runs the passkey challenge, the primary credential. Kratos sets the session cookie and resumes at Hydra, which opens a consent challenge; the app gates again, composes id_token claims with groups mapped to claims, and accepts. Hydra redirects the browser back to Grafana with a code, exchanged server-side for tokens: signed in. A remembered subject takes a different first step, re-verifying a live Kratos session before honouring Hydra's skip.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">One code exchange, four parties, one live Kratos session underneath it</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The apps that speak no OIDC</h2>
    <p>
      Not every application in the house can run an OAuth2 redirect, so the
      edge asks a question instead: a sub-request to the portal's
      forward-auth endpoint, carrying the session cookie and a group name
      the edge itself configured. The endpoint answers with a status, not a
      redirect, and the status has to mean one thing consistently or the
      edge cannot safely act on it. 401 is the only denial an edge can turn
      into a login redirect; 503 means the identity service never
      answered, and folding that into 403 would turn an outage into an
      infinite bounce back to a login page that cannot help.
      <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The forward-auth request path has the
      full status contract; the <a href="/guides/per-app-oidc/">Per-app OIDC</a> guide's When it is
      not OIDC section shows it wired to a real proxy.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/forward-auth-request-path.svg" alt="A browser carrying the apex-scoped session cookie reaches the edge, which routes at L4 by SNI without decrypting, then the service's own proxy — the door, where forward_auth runs before any reverse_proxy. The proxy issues a sub-request to the portal's forward-auth endpoint with the required group as a query parameter set by the edge config, never an inbound header. The portal verifies the session cookie, re-resolves the identity through the Kratos admin API because group metadata is absent from whoami, and applies enforceGroupGate — identity active, every stored group canonical, and in the named group — failing closed at every step. It answers one of four statuses: 200 proxy through with the identity header set from the server-verified session; 401 no usable session, the one status an edge can safely turn into a login redirect; 403 refused on the merits; and 503 when Kratos did not answer, which denies like a 403 but must never be matched into a redirect. The application sees only the identity header.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">One sub-request decides the request, before the app ever sees it</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>One group gets you in; the other decides what you can do</h2>
    <p>
      Every manifest binds a group twice, and the two bindings do
      different jobs on purpose. <code>presentation.access</code> is
      entry: hold it and the gate admits you, full stop.
      <code>consent.group_role_map</code> is role: it elevates someone
      already admitted and grants nothing on its own, so holding only an
      app's admin group gets a person nowhere. Keeping those two questions
      on two separate bindings is what makes an admin group safe to have
      at all; collapsing them is the shortcut this model exists to close
      off. The <a href="/guides/per-app-oidc/">Per-app OIDC</a> guide's
      Entry and role are different bindings section has the manifest
      shape; <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The access model has the
      resolution order.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/group-binding-decision.svg" alt="Three columns. The identity holds groups in admin-API-only metadata. The manifest binds them twice: presentation.access is the entry binding, consent.group_role_map is the role binding. The code then decides — the access gate admits on the entry binding, and lookupRole resolves a role from the highest-ranked match. Holding only the admin group admits nobody, and a group no manifest binds grants nothing.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">The identity holds · the manifest binds · the code decides</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What actually happens on sign-out</h2>
    <p>
      Hydra and Kratos each remember something about a session, and
      cutting only one of them leaves the other able to mint a login on
      its own. Revoking a subject is one in-process fan-out that cuts the
      Kratos session and both of Hydra's remembered login and consent
      sessions together, in a fixed order, whether it was triggered by the
      person signing out everywhere, an admin forcing a logout, or a
      deactivation. It stops sessions and remembered logins; it does not
      remove enrolled credentials, which is why a lost-device eviction is
      a separate procedure that deletes the credential first. The <a href="/guides/hardening/">Hardening</a> guide's Sessions and
      eviction section has that procedure; <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The OIDC path has the revocation
      fan-out.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/session-revocation.svg" alt="Two panels. Left: when Hydra reports skip=true because it remembers a subject, the login handler re-verifies that a live Kratos session exists whose identity.id equals the remembered subject before honouring the skip; no live match forces interactive re-authentication, pinned by the FIX-A cases in portal/scripts/oauth2-handlers.test.mjs. Right: revokeSubject is the one in-process revocation fan-out, triggered by admin force-logout, sign-out-everywhere, or deactivate. Kratos sessions, Hydra's login-session and Hydra's consent-session are all revoked in a fixed order; this cuts sessions and remembered logins but not enrolled credentials, which is why the full eviction procedure above audits credentials and deletes the affected type before it revokes.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">Hydra remembers; Kratos authenticates — and revoking cuts both</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>How the first credential gets in the door</h2>
    <p>
      There is no self-service registration, so every identity starts with
      an admin creating it through the admin API with no credentials block
      at all. What reaches the new person is a recovery link and a
      one-time code, delivered over two channels that both keep the
      password, or passkey, itself off the wire. That is the mechanism
      behind a rule stated once and held everywhere it matters: no
      password ever transits a channel. The <a href="/guides/golden-path/">Golden path</a> guide's Registration
      is admin-driven section has the operator side; <code>DECISIONS.md</code> § No password ever transits a channel has
      the reasoning.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/first-credential.svg" alt="An admin creates the identity through the admin API with no credentials block at all. Two channels can then carry the enrolment secret, and both keep link and code apart from any password. Hand-delivery: the admin mints a one-time recovery link and code together and hands both over on a conversation that already exists with that person. Email, the D1 addendum: the admin re-types the target address and the server checks it byte-equal against the identity's own trait before anything is sent; Kratos's own courier then sends a code alone, never a link with the code embedded. Either way the recipient's own browser is where a password and a passkey first get set. One branch refuses outright: when the target carries the admin marker, shouldRefuseRecovery refuses before Kratos is ever asked, console-only.">
      <figcaption class="diagram-caption">Not "no password exists" — no password ever transits a channel</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      That is the shape of it end to end. <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> has the rest of the reasoning, <code>DECISIONS.md</code> has the
      roads not taken, and the <a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/othala">repository</a> has the
      code and the tests these diagrams are drawn from.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
